WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:07.950
RYAN MCDOWELL: Welcome to
Google Cloud Next OnAir.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.450
In this session,
we'll be discussing

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.880
Major League Baseball's
data warehouse modernization

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.590
journey.

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:17.160
I'm Ryan McDowell, Cloud
Data Engineer from Google.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.990
And joining me today
is Rob Goretsky,

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:23.220
VP of Data Engineering
at Major League Baseball.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:26.220
Today, we'll be taking a look
at general migration strategy

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.760
for cloud data warehouse
migrations and then deep dive

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.210
into how and why Major League
Baseball moved their data

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:34.190
warehouse to BigQuery.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.890
Starting off with the overall
strategy of data warehouse

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:45.550
migrations to the cloud,
we've heard from our customers

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:48.790
that traditional data warehouses
are causing tons of pain,

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:53.040
as they are unable to keep up
with today's analytic demand.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.280
There's tremendous complexity
with scaling on demand.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.860
And this is compounded
by the inability

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:02.190
of their incumbent technology
to keep up with data growth.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:05.760
Traditional data warehouses
were built for batch paradigms

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.550
and not for today's
world, where, by 2025,

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:11.670
about one fourth of data
will be streaming in nature.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.470
Access restrictions
and data silos

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:16.980
on-premise inhibit data
sharing and collaboration.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:20.100
Existing legacy solutions
have the inability

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.280
to take advantage of emerging
technologies such as AI

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.500
and real time.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:27.300
And you need to move data
out of your data warehouse

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.180
into complex, fragile,
or niche systems that

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.500
require PhDs to operate.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.840
Finally, legacy
data warehouses are

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.940
extremely expensive to
purchase and maintain.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:41.760
So how does BigQuery
address these challenges?

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.930
BigQuery allows you to
store vast amounts of data.

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:46.970
With BigQuery,
Telegraph Media Group

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:49.730
reduced their query time
from hours to minutes

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:52.010
with up to 4 terabytes
of data processed

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:54.120
in less than one minute.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:58.610
Blue Apron streams 140 million
events per day with BigQuery.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:00.740
This has helped them
accelerate business decisions

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:04.850
by one day to near real
time to better manage

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:07.460
food inventory and delivery.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.070
Home Depot is able to
empower business units

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:11.900
to create their own
BigQuery projects

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.980
and reduce dependency on IT
budgeting for additional data

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:17.960
warehouse capacity.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:21.350
20th Century Fox uses BigQuery
to run analytics to pull data

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:24.650
from DoubleClick and
YouTube, and uses TensorFlow

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:27.200
to do audience analysis
on film scripts

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.900
and track content patterns
that may better predict

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.390
what resonates with audiences.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:35.780
HSBC needed to cost effectively
upgrade their ageing Teradata

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:40.160
environment and across multiple
regulatory jurisdictions.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.770
By moving to BigQuery, they
were able to save millions over

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:43.898
seven years.

00:02:43.898 --> 00:02:46.190
So how should you be thinking
about your data warehouse

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:48.670
modernization strategy?

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:51.970
This diagram shows a high
level legacy architecture

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:53.640
from before the migration.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:57.490
It illustrates the catalog of
available data sources, legacy

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:00.700
data pipelines, legacy
operational pipelines,

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:02.260
and feedback loops.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:04.990
Additionally, legacy BI
reports in the dashboards that

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:07.540
are accessed by your end users.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:10.330
In order to migrate a legacy
data warehouse architecture

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:13.330
to BigQuery, we first need
to go about decomposing

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:15.130
the migration, such
that we aren't left

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:18.170
with a risky big bang cutover.

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:21.460
At Google Cloud, we generally
think about data warehouse

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:24.580
migrations through the lens
of the following framework.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:26.260
First, through
cloud foundations,

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:29.110
where we set the foundational
cloud infrastructure including

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:31.450
network, IM, and security.

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:33.430
Then risk assessment
and mitigation,

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:35.860
where we evaluate
the operational risk

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:38.840
of the migration and then
develop detailed mitigation

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:40.320
plans.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.480
Third, the migration,
where we actually

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.830
execute the migration
comprising of various steps.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:48.870
Then change management
and learning,

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:50.280
setting up a center
of excellence

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.770
within your organization
such that when

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:55.140
the new environment
is cut over, users

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:57.360
are confident from day one.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.490
During the migration,
we generally approach it

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:01.020
from an iterative standpoint.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:03.150
First, we'll assess
and plan the migration

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:06.870
based on the source environment
and design the destination.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.420
Then we'll work through
an iterative process going

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:12.600
workload by workload or
subject area by subject area,

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:16.649
where we work on migrating
those workloads from the source

00:04:16.649 --> 00:04:18.839
system to the
destination system.

00:04:18.839 --> 00:04:21.839
This generally comprises of
enterprise setup and security

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:26.070
mapping, migrating schema
and data using BigQuery DTS,

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:29.220
translating queries using
some of our partner tools,

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:33.330
migrating business apps,
migrating upstream pipelines,

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:35.370
verification and
validation to ensure

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:37.410
that once the
environment is cut over,

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.660
that users are getting
the same results on top

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:43.770
of that same data sets that
they had on their source system,

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:46.620
performance tuning, and then
of course report and scale

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:48.795
testing.

00:04:48.795 --> 00:04:50.420
Now I'll turn it over
to Rob to take us

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:52.700
through how Major League
Baseball completed

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:55.875
their migration journey.

00:04:55.875 --> 00:04:57.250
ROBERT GORETSKY:
Thank you, Ryan.

00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:00.070
My name's Rob Goretsky, and
I'm the VP of Data Engineering

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:01.330
at Major League Baseball.

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:03.580
Today, I'm going to talk
about Major League Baseball's

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:06.310
migration from Teradata
to BigQuery, including

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:09.130
the motivations for migration,
the actual migration

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:12.250
process itself, and the
capabilities we unlocked when

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:14.850
we completed this migration.

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:17.270
So as a bit of a background,
I oversee the fan data

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:19.130
engineering and business
intelligence teams

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:20.630
at Major League baseball.

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:22.850
We work with fan
transaction and interaction

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:26.360
data generated by internally
developed applications,

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:29.990
including mlb.com and the 30
Major League Baseball club

00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:33.200
websites, and our MLB
and ballpark apps,

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:36.030
and third party sites such
as Ticketmaster, tickets.com,

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:38.690
StubHub, and Fanatics.

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:40.880
My team doesn't work
directly with player stats

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:43.107
or on field data,
such as Statcast.

00:05:43.107 --> 00:05:44.690
That's the domain
of our baseball data

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:46.250
team at Major League Baseball.

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:48.620
But we do work with the more
traditional user engagement

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:50.660
data to ensure that
we understand our fans

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:53.210
and can bring them closer
to the game of baseball.

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:55.850
This includes personalizing
and targeting marketing and fan

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:58.460
experiences on our
website and on our apps

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:01.190
and sharing our centralized
data with all 30 Major League

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:06.350
Baseball clubs in our
project known as Wheelhouse.

00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:08.480
We have over 350
data pipelines that

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:11.930
have been developed over
the last 10 plus years.

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:13.430
Most of our data
sets are not what I

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:15.660
consider to be really big data.

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:18.740
For example, a data set
containing every ticket sale

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:20.570
only contains about
80 million rows

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:23.390
per year for a couple
hundred gigabytes of data.

00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:26.630
That said, we do have a
few very large data sets

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:30.182
generated by user interactions
on our website and apps.

00:06:30.182 --> 00:06:31.640
These are highly
trafficked, and we

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:33.910
collect every user
interaction and store it

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:36.020
in our data warehouse platform.

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:38.420
In addition, many of the
medium-sized data sets,

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:41.450
such as ticket sales, turn
out to be pretty complicated

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:43.700
in terms of the
modeling and validation

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:47.450
due to the various ways fans
can purchase, exchange, resell,

00:06:47.450 --> 00:06:52.135
transfer, and ultimately
share and use these items.

00:06:52.135 --> 00:06:53.510
Before our migration
to BigQuery,

00:06:53.510 --> 00:06:56.660
we used Teradata as our
data warehouse platform.

00:06:56.660 --> 00:06:59.780
We ran data pipelines using
Informatica and Airflow.

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:02.330
MLB employees could
access Teradata directly

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:06.110
by connecting using SQL and
using our BI platform, Looker.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:08.450
But we didn't have a means
to open up access directly

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:11.450
to Teradata for all 30
Major League Baseball clubs

00:07:11.450 --> 00:07:14.520
without major performance
and security implications.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:17.630
So we set up Airflow pipelines
to essentially copy data

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:22.190
from Teradata to S3 in 30
separate files, one per club

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:24.530
per data set, to
allow clubs to access

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:27.440
that data via S3 and Athena.

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:30.410
This setup presented
several challenges.

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:33.050
The major challenge with
sharing data with clubs

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:35.025
was that we were copying data.

00:07:35.025 --> 00:07:37.400
So when we're copying data,
you need to properly identify

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:39.230
which date has changed
each day if you

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:41.020
want to do an incremental copy.

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:42.410
And this was
challenging in cases

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:45.200
where we had late arriving
data or corrections to make

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:47.720
to the data that happened
often after the fact, which

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:50.690
was especially common with
our ticketing data sets.

00:07:50.690 --> 00:07:53.450
In addition, if we wanted
to evolve the schema that we

00:07:53.450 --> 00:07:56.000
shared with clubs, either by
adding fields or changing data

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:58.010
types, that often
would then require

00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:00.303
us to recopy all
historical data out

00:08:00.303 --> 00:08:01.970
to the club, which
can be time consuming

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:04.020
and error prone itself.

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:06.002
In addition, if we had
a correction to make,

00:08:06.002 --> 00:08:08.210
we often would need to
circulate that on a change log

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:09.835
so that clubs would
be aware that we've

00:08:09.835 --> 00:08:11.360
made a change to
the data, that they

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:13.010
should re-fetch
perhaps older data

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:15.140
files that we have published.

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:17.858
This process was also very
much a one-way street.

00:08:17.858 --> 00:08:19.400
Clubs didn't have
a way to share data

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:22.160
back with us in a way
that we could easily query

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:26.180
and that would automatically set
up schemas for us to work with.

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:29.910
As our data sources started to
become more and more real time,

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:31.310
another issue with
this setup was

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:34.490
that the system to share
data was itself batch-based.

00:08:34.490 --> 00:08:37.159
So even if we were
ingesting data in real time,

00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:38.600
to share the data
back with clubs

00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:41.450
involved making a copy
perhaps every hour,

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:43.700
or even more frequently,
every five minutes.

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:45.560
But we never really
hit a real time cadence

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:48.620
where clubs could see
the data as it happened.

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:50.540
Other major challenges
outside of the data

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:52.280
sharing with working
with Teradata

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:54.920
included the operational
support overhead.

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:57.147
We maintained a
full time DBA team,

00:08:57.147 --> 00:08:58.730
who helped us with
the traditional DBA

00:08:58.730 --> 00:09:02.180
tasks such as resolving
performance issues,

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:05.510
monitoring for database dead
locks and long running queries,

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:06.998
resolving backup issues.

00:09:06.998 --> 00:09:08.540
And we spent a lot
of time and effort

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:11.180
trying to continue to refine
that part of the system

00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:12.890
when really that wasn't
our core concern.

00:09:12.890 --> 00:09:15.320
Our core concern was ultimately
providing business data

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:18.368
and analytics to
our organization.

00:09:18.368 --> 00:09:19.910
With respect to
performance, we often

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:22.430
found that in spite
of our best efforts

00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:25.250
to implement proper workload
management and tuning,

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:28.100
we often would have one bad
apple spoiling the bunch.

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:30.320
A single well-intentioned
user query

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:32.000
that ate up the
entire spool space

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:34.215
or otherwise brought
the system to its knees

00:09:34.215 --> 00:09:35.840
was fairly common
and something that we

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:38.432
had to deal with all the time.

00:09:38.432 --> 00:09:39.890
Another issue as
we started looking

00:09:39.890 --> 00:09:41.510
at scaling our
system on Teradata

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:44.313
was that the compute and
storage were tightly coupled.

00:09:44.313 --> 00:09:45.980
This meant that if
we wanted to consider

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:48.260
bringing in some of the
very, very large data sets,

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:50.930
such as our clickstream,
we couldn't do so

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:53.720
without also linearly
increasing our compute, which

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:55.880
came at much additional
cost that perhaps didn't

00:09:55.880 --> 00:09:58.178
make sense for us overall.

00:09:58.178 --> 00:10:00.470
Furthermore, we found that
integration with third party

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:02.540
tools and apps was limited.

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:04.520
When we were looking to
integrate with new BI

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:07.420
platforms, or data science
tools or analytics tools,

00:10:07.420 --> 00:10:10.280
often their support for
Teradata was mediocre at best.

00:10:12.830 --> 00:10:16.880
So we ran a project to fully
migrate Teradata from BigQuery

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:19.130
from May through November 2019.

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:22.883
So we ran this entire project
in about seven months time.

00:10:22.883 --> 00:10:24.300
At a high level,
the work involved

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:27.170
setting up data replications
from Teradata to BigQuery

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:29.690
and then iterating through
the conversion of ETL jobs

00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:30.980
and reports.

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:33.470
We needed to continue to
support live reporting

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:37.070
and agreed that a big bang
cutover happening all at once

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:39.860
was not going to be feasible
or desirable for us.

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:41.990
We instead wanted to
take things in chunks

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:43.610
and ensure that
along the way, we

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:46.550
were validating that BigQuery
was performing as expected

00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:49.400
and that data was loading
properly and matching up

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:51.750
with our expectations.

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:53.960
So in order to determine
the order in which

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:58.010
to attack converting and
migrating all of our ETL jobs,

00:10:58.010 --> 00:11:00.200
we built a dependency
map that took

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:02.690
a look at which tables
and ETL jobs were upstream

00:11:02.690 --> 00:11:04.450
and downstream from each other.

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:06.950
Jobs that were less entangled,
because they were on the leaf

00:11:06.950 --> 00:11:10.490
nodes in this diagram, had
fewer downstream dependencies,

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:12.620
and therefore could
be migrated first.

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:14.930
So we worked outside in, if
you will, on this diagram,

00:11:14.930 --> 00:11:16.388
where we took those
things that had

00:11:16.388 --> 00:11:19.100
the fewest downstream
dependencies, migrated

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:23.750
them first, and then continued
to work our way inwards.

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:25.520
We set up hundreds
of replication jobs

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:28.040
using Apache Airflow very
early on in our project

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:31.392
and throughout the project as
needed as new tables arose.

00:11:31.392 --> 00:11:33.350
One of the more time
consuming tasks about this

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:35.330
was determining
exactly which upstream

00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:38.030
process we should wait for
before replicating data

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:39.030
to BigQuery.

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:41.390
But we considered this
critical for our success

00:11:41.390 --> 00:11:43.790
because we want the
BigQuery to be as close

00:11:43.790 --> 00:11:47.120
as possible to being a perfect
match of what was in Teradata.

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:50.280
So for example, if we get a
daily load of StubHub data

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:53.450
and that arrived into
Teradata at 9:00 AM,

00:11:53.450 --> 00:11:56.630
we were hoping that we could
have it in BigQuery by 9:01 AM.

00:11:56.630 --> 00:11:58.370
So this is not a
hot, hot replication.

00:11:58.370 --> 00:12:00.770
It's not necessarily what you
might consider a real time

00:12:00.770 --> 00:12:01.730
replication.

00:12:01.730 --> 00:12:03.500
But it was basically
close enough

00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:06.860
that all of our analysts, data
scientists, report builders, BI

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:09.620
team could immediately
start using BigQuery

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:12.560
very early on in the project
for all of their new work.

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:15.590
This ensured, basically, that
all the data was essentially

00:12:15.590 --> 00:12:18.560
in BigQuery as fresh
as it was in Teradata.

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:22.490
So as a result of this carefully
timed setup for replication,

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:24.350
these data
stakeholder teams were

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:26.870
able to start developing
new queries and processes

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:29.000
on BigQuery very early on.

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:30.650
This was great
because otherwise,

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:33.590
if teams were continuing to
build new work on Teradata

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:35.402
during the seven
month migration,

00:12:35.402 --> 00:12:37.610
we might have another seven
months worth of migration

00:12:37.610 --> 00:12:40.123
to do afterwards, and we might
never finish the project.

00:12:40.123 --> 00:12:41.540
So it was important
for us that we

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:43.550
could draw a line
in the sand and say

00:12:43.550 --> 00:12:45.590
to all of these
stakeholder teams,

00:12:45.590 --> 00:12:47.302
only build new
things on BigQuery.

00:12:47.302 --> 00:12:49.010
We're going to replicate
all the data you

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:50.330
need to work on your process.

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:52.080
So for example, for
the data science team,

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:54.163
we're going to make sure
that all the ticket sales

00:12:54.163 --> 00:12:56.270
data that you need to
do predictive analytics

00:12:56.270 --> 00:12:57.860
is there in BigQuery.

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:01.070
So it's going to look and feel
just like it does on Teradata,

00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:02.837
but be available to
you at the same time

00:13:02.837 --> 00:13:05.420
so that everything new you build
today, you can start building

00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:06.590
on BigQuery.

00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:08.720
In the back end, we
continued migrating the ETL

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:11.300
jobs themselves, but by
simply having replication

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:13.040
it unlocked the
ability for most teams

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:15.380
to think about BigQuery as
a live production system

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:16.910
right away.

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:19.550
As we converted ETL jobs,
having replication in place

00:13:19.550 --> 00:13:23.030
also allowed us to ensure that
we could do table validation

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:25.160
and match results between
the result of output

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:28.330
from Teradata and BigQuery.

00:13:28.330 --> 00:13:30.280
So in order to proceed
with ETL conversion,

00:13:30.280 --> 00:13:32.260
we focused on
developing a recipe that

00:13:32.260 --> 00:13:35.080
can be followed
by data engineers

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:37.750
without them having
advance knowledge of any

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:40.150
of the business or domain
logic that were contained

00:13:40.150 --> 00:13:41.840
within that pipeline.

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:44.760
So we started off looking at
steps that could be automated.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:46.990
And we spent some time
upfront developing tooling

00:13:46.990 --> 00:13:48.920
to automate those processes.

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:50.920
For example, we wrote
shell or Python scripts

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:54.725
that would simply pull pieces
of SQL out of parts of our code

00:13:54.725 --> 00:13:56.350
so that we could
quickly identify which

00:13:56.350 --> 00:13:58.510
pieces needed to be translated.

00:13:58.510 --> 00:14:00.010
We also looked for
cases where there

00:14:00.010 --> 00:14:02.770
were Teradata specific
function calls or Teradata

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:05.360
specific commands being issued.

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:07.360
And we were able to pull
those out and translate

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:08.410
those en mass.

00:14:08.410 --> 00:14:10.990
So this meant that for the first
month or so of our project,

00:14:10.990 --> 00:14:13.810
we weren't necessarily doing
an end-to-end ETL conversion

00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:14.410
just yet.

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:15.983
But we were building
out the tools

00:14:15.983 --> 00:14:18.400
that would enable us to then
move very quickly through all

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:20.650
of the others.

00:14:20.650 --> 00:14:23.597
So once we got through building
out a lot of the automation,

00:14:23.597 --> 00:14:25.680
the remaining steps was a
majority of the project,

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:28.170
and it was done by
our data engineers.

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:30.150
This involved places
where either it

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:32.850
was too difficult to build
automation for something,

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:36.327
or because we wanted to have
validation done by a human who

00:14:36.327 --> 00:14:38.160
could look over something
and ensure that it

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:40.830
passed proper sanity checks.

00:14:40.830 --> 00:14:42.300
Also working with
Informatica, we

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:45.505
found that there was no easy
way to automate mass changes.

00:14:45.505 --> 00:14:47.880
So a lot of those involved
clicking through the interface

00:14:47.880 --> 00:14:51.600
and just manually making
changes in the GUI.

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:53.820
So with respect to
automated components,

00:14:53.820 --> 00:14:55.230
one tool that
helped us immensely

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:59.370
was a SQL transpiler tool by a
company called CompilerWorks.

00:14:59.370 --> 00:15:02.190
This tool accepts
Teradata SQL as input

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:05.270
and then outputs BigQuery SQL.

00:15:05.270 --> 00:15:08.270
So while minor SQL differences,
such as function name changes

00:15:08.270 --> 00:15:10.940
or small syntax changes,
could have been resolved

00:15:10.940 --> 00:15:13.460
by a simple regex
search and replace,

00:15:13.460 --> 00:15:15.410
more complex cases
involve having

00:15:15.410 --> 00:15:17.300
a deeper understanding
of the SQL structure

00:15:17.300 --> 00:15:19.247
and the data types
that underlie them.

00:15:19.247 --> 00:15:21.830
Here's a really common case that
we had that used the Teradata

00:15:21.830 --> 00:15:23.715
specific Qualify clause.

00:15:23.715 --> 00:15:25.340
The CompilerWorks
tool was able to pull

00:15:25.340 --> 00:15:28.130
the clause used within
Qualify and rewrite

00:15:28.130 --> 00:15:30.110
the query as an inner query.

00:15:30.110 --> 00:15:33.090
Doing this at hand would have
been error prone at best.

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:35.840
Another major advantage we found
when using the CompilerWorks

00:15:35.840 --> 00:15:39.210
tool is that it became aware
of the actual schema itself,

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:40.890
including the data types.

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:43.440
So BigQuery is pretty
specific about data types.

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:46.125
For example, if a field
as a data type of integer,

00:15:46.125 --> 00:15:47.750
it may not allow you
to directly insert

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:50.480
a numeric value without
casting it to an integer.

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:52.317
Teradata was a bit
more lax about this.

00:15:52.317 --> 00:15:54.650
So this is the kind of thing
that a translation normally

00:15:54.650 --> 00:15:56.600
would trip up on
time and time again.

00:15:56.600 --> 00:15:58.850
The CompilerWorks's tool,
being aware of the data

00:15:58.850 --> 00:16:01.970
type of a column, when it
parsed and read the SQL it

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:03.680
was actually able to
automatically apply

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:06.475
the proper cast or conversion
statement to it for us

00:16:06.475 --> 00:16:07.850
so that the syntax
would be valid

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:10.950
and would run immediately.

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:13.020
When we were done
converting an ETL pipeline,

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:15.370
we continue running the
original pipeline in Teradata.

00:16:15.370 --> 00:16:17.370
But we'd run the converted
pipeline side-by-side

00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:19.740
in a prod parallel environment.

00:16:19.740 --> 00:16:23.640
In this diagram here, we
see the Airflow DAG diagram

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:26.550
of our original ETL
with Teradata operators

00:16:26.550 --> 00:16:27.840
embedded within.

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:30.720
And on the right hand side,
we see the exact same pipeline

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:33.060
with BigQuery operators
embedded within.

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:35.940
And we set these up to run
in two separate environments,

00:16:35.940 --> 00:16:39.100
but generally at the same time
of day with the same input data

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:41.880
so that we could basically
do a side-by-side check

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:45.500
that the output
data would match.

00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:47.818
To further assist us with
this table validation,

00:16:47.818 --> 00:16:50.360
we looked in the marketplace
for tools that would essentially

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:53.090
allow us to run a diff
between two tables,

00:16:53.090 --> 00:16:54.770
similar to how you
run a diff when

00:16:54.770 --> 00:16:59.113
you're using Git to look at code
review or pull request reviews.

00:16:59.113 --> 00:17:01.280
We couldn't find anything
that fit our needs exactly

00:17:01.280 --> 00:17:05.540
in terms of allowing us to do
a table-to-table comparison

00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:08.300
by joining on the primary
key and then telling us

00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:10.700
if rows were missing
from one table or another

00:17:10.700 --> 00:17:12.230
or if rows had
fields that differed

00:17:12.230 --> 00:17:13.770
in one table or another.

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:15.109
So we built our own tool.

00:17:15.109 --> 00:17:17.060
Our own tool,
essentially, ran queries

00:17:17.060 --> 00:17:19.670
and generated SQL
for us on BigQuery

00:17:19.670 --> 00:17:22.609
to take the output of the
replicated Teradata ETL

00:17:22.609 --> 00:17:26.240
and compare that with the
output of the BigQuery ETL.

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:28.310
This would quickly
surface to us if there

00:17:28.310 --> 00:17:30.350
were either differences
in the row count,

00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:32.930
or in rows missing on
one side or the other,

00:17:32.930 --> 00:17:34.955
or if there were
field differences.

00:17:34.955 --> 00:17:36.830
At this point, this was
one of the few pieces

00:17:36.830 --> 00:17:39.410
of the project that really
required domain and analyst

00:17:39.410 --> 00:17:42.640
expertise, because if the
diff tool showed differences--

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:44.588
and it often would
show some differences--

00:17:44.588 --> 00:17:46.130
we needed to make
a business decision

00:17:46.130 --> 00:17:48.110
about whether those
differences in the output

00:17:48.110 --> 00:17:49.977
were relevant for our use case.

00:17:49.977 --> 00:17:51.560
For example, if there
was a field that

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:53.778
simply had to create
a date or a timestamp,

00:17:53.778 --> 00:17:55.820
we would expect those
would be slightly different

00:17:55.820 --> 00:17:57.713
between Teradata
and BigQuery, simply

00:17:57.713 --> 00:17:59.630
because of the slightly
different runtime when

00:17:59.630 --> 00:18:01.370
those jobs completed.

00:18:01.370 --> 00:18:04.010
However, if there was a field
such as a financial charge

00:18:04.010 --> 00:18:06.260
value, we would expect
that generally those

00:18:06.260 --> 00:18:07.638
should match up exactly.

00:18:07.638 --> 00:18:10.180
So if there was a difference in
those and it was significant,

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:12.830
you would need to dig in further
to see where in the pipeline

00:18:12.830 --> 00:18:14.780
something was
either mistranslated

00:18:14.780 --> 00:18:17.910
or something perhaps was thrown
off for some other reason.

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:19.580
So having a tool
like this allowed

00:18:19.580 --> 00:18:22.960
us to have the data engineers
spit out conformed output

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:25.580
that then we could pass
to analysts and end users,

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:29.090
and more experienced users of
the business analytics side

00:18:29.090 --> 00:18:32.390
to help us understand
what the differences were.

00:18:32.390 --> 00:18:34.460
So taking a look again
at where we went,

00:18:34.460 --> 00:18:36.740
we took a look before
at our data architecture

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:40.040
before our BigQuery migration,
including this middle layer

00:18:40.040 --> 00:18:44.180
where we had landing data as
a copy for club data access

00:18:44.180 --> 00:18:46.340
in our Wheelhouse project.

00:18:46.340 --> 00:18:47.870
After BigQuery
migration, you see

00:18:47.870 --> 00:18:50.690
that we've removed that
middle layer completely.

00:18:50.690 --> 00:18:54.230
Consumers of data, whether
they are at clubs outside

00:18:54.230 --> 00:18:58.640
of our network, or internal
users, or data science,

00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:00.410
or other constituent
teams, are all

00:19:00.410 --> 00:19:02.870
looking at the exact
same copy of the data.

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:05.675
There is not a second
copy made of any data.

00:19:05.675 --> 00:19:07.550
This means that if we
load data in real time,

00:19:07.550 --> 00:19:10.460
it's available to those
consumers in real time.

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:11.960
And it also means
that we don't have

00:19:11.960 --> 00:19:13.585
to deal with all the
discrepancies that

00:19:13.585 --> 00:19:16.068
arise by making copies of data.

00:19:16.068 --> 00:19:17.860
So we mentioned a few
of the benefits here.

00:19:17.860 --> 00:19:19.990
And we're going to
summarize them here.

00:19:19.990 --> 00:19:21.790
With respect to
democratization of data,

00:19:21.790 --> 00:19:24.280
I will do a live demo
here in a few moments

00:19:24.280 --> 00:19:26.950
to show how we could use
the BigQuery tools to very

00:19:26.950 --> 00:19:29.440
easily share data
and securely ensure

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:31.090
that we only share
a subset of data

00:19:31.090 --> 00:19:33.850
with the proper stakeholders.

00:19:33.850 --> 00:19:35.560
With respect to
performance and scale,

00:19:35.560 --> 00:19:37.900
we saw that some of
our large queries

00:19:37.900 --> 00:19:40.240
had a 50% reduction in
runtime without really

00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:42.430
any additional work
done on our end.

00:19:42.430 --> 00:19:44.470
And similarly, we saw
that certain data sets,

00:19:44.470 --> 00:19:46.240
such as our
clickstream data, which

00:19:46.240 --> 00:19:49.780
consists of over 300 terabytes
a year in uncompressed data,

00:19:49.780 --> 00:19:52.420
were able to be loaded into
BigQuery, whereas previously we

00:19:52.420 --> 00:19:54.563
couldn't even consider
doing something like that.

00:19:54.563 --> 00:19:56.480
So we instead needed to
keep really large data

00:19:56.480 --> 00:19:59.800
sets like those in separate
silos within a Hadoop ecosystem

00:19:59.800 --> 00:20:00.925
to process them.

00:20:00.925 --> 00:20:03.550
Having them all in one place was
one of the single biggest user

00:20:03.550 --> 00:20:05.860
requests that we had
gotten, because users often

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:08.170
want to join the clickstream
data with other data sets,

00:20:08.170 --> 00:20:11.410
such as our ticketing
or shop data.

00:20:11.410 --> 00:20:13.630
For security, instead of
having to have separate user

00:20:13.630 --> 00:20:15.760
rights and roles and
credentials, all of this

00:20:15.760 --> 00:20:18.100
is centrally managed
by our InfoSec team

00:20:18.100 --> 00:20:20.800
by way of our integration
with Okta and G Suite.

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:22.780
So users are set up
with groups within Okta.

00:20:22.780 --> 00:20:24.550
And those flow
automatically into G Suite,

00:20:24.550 --> 00:20:26.710
which then flows automatically
into BigQuery Roles

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:27.872
and Responsibilities.

00:20:27.872 --> 00:20:29.830
This means we don't have
to have any time spent

00:20:29.830 --> 00:20:32.200
on setting passwords,
worrying about who

00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:34.000
has compromised access,
who might otherwise

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:35.230
leave the organization.

00:20:35.230 --> 00:20:39.190
All of it is set up
centrally in one place.

00:20:39.190 --> 00:20:41.200
Developer happiness is
really a key one for me

00:20:41.200 --> 00:20:42.642
and my team, in
that we just found

00:20:42.642 --> 00:20:44.350
that everybody who
worked with the system

00:20:44.350 --> 00:20:46.180
understood things
right away with it

00:20:46.180 --> 00:20:48.790
and generally had less
gotchas than what they did

00:20:48.790 --> 00:20:50.260
with Teradata.

00:20:50.260 --> 00:20:52.900
More specifically, we found that
one of the biggest advantages

00:20:52.900 --> 00:20:55.240
is that we can often
Google if we're not

00:20:55.240 --> 00:20:57.700
sure about exactly how
to do something and find

00:20:57.700 --> 00:21:00.490
a helpful Stack Overflow
answer almost right away.

00:21:00.490 --> 00:21:02.890
This wasn't necessarily
the case with Teradata,

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:05.675
which was generally the
domain of enterprise companies

00:21:05.675 --> 00:21:07.300
and larger customers,
and really wasn't

00:21:07.300 --> 00:21:09.250
as widely available
to the masses

00:21:09.250 --> 00:21:10.943
as something like BigQuery is.

00:21:10.943 --> 00:21:12.610
Where with BigQuery,
there's a free tier

00:21:12.610 --> 00:21:15.770
that any engineer can jump in
and try things out with it.

00:21:15.770 --> 00:21:18.000
It just means that there is
a wider mindshare for it.

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:19.780
It means that there's more
documentation and more

00:21:19.780 --> 00:21:20.920
community support for it.

00:21:20.920 --> 00:21:23.885
And we find that helpful
time and time again.

00:21:23.885 --> 00:21:25.510
With respect to
features, we're finding

00:21:25.510 --> 00:21:27.580
that features are consistently
added that help us

00:21:27.580 --> 00:21:29.480
out without breaking
anything for us

00:21:29.480 --> 00:21:32.680
and without requiring any
downtime for upgrades.

00:21:32.680 --> 00:21:35.020
With Teradata, we were
pinned to a specific version

00:21:35.020 --> 00:21:37.420
for several years at a
time because an upgrade

00:21:37.420 --> 00:21:39.130
was a major process.

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:41.260
For us with BigQuery,
we just take a look

00:21:41.260 --> 00:21:43.400
at our system one morning
and we see release notes

00:21:43.400 --> 00:21:44.670
and there's new features.

00:21:44.670 --> 00:21:46.087
And it's like
opening a goody bag.

00:21:46.087 --> 00:21:50.110
You immediately have some new
toys to play with every day.

00:21:50.110 --> 00:21:52.420
So with respect
to flexibility, we

00:21:52.420 --> 00:21:54.135
found that early
on in our testing,

00:21:54.135 --> 00:21:56.260
we didn't want to make a
big commitment about going

00:21:56.260 --> 00:21:56.997
with BigQuery.

00:21:56.997 --> 00:21:59.080
But we loved that we could
use the on-demand model

00:21:59.080 --> 00:22:01.180
to load some data, even
some of our larger data

00:22:01.180 --> 00:22:03.100
sets, and try things
out without having

00:22:03.100 --> 00:22:05.800
to make any upfront commitment.

00:22:05.800 --> 00:22:09.160
The operational overhead has
been significantly reduced.

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:10.720
Right now backups are automatic.

00:22:10.720 --> 00:22:12.910
And one of the things
that our team really loves

00:22:12.910 --> 00:22:14.710
is the ability to
simply ask BigQuery

00:22:14.710 --> 00:22:17.365
to present a copy of a table
anytime in the last seven days.

00:22:17.365 --> 00:22:19.990
So if you're trying to validate
a data load issue that happened

00:22:19.990 --> 00:22:21.970
last night, you can
simply ask BigQuery

00:22:21.970 --> 00:22:24.620
to present the copy of
the table from yesterday.

00:22:24.620 --> 00:22:26.410
And you could take a
look at what changed.

00:22:26.410 --> 00:22:28.810
Similarly, if you've
ever accidentally dropped

00:22:28.810 --> 00:22:32.230
a production table, and I know
none of us have ever done that,

00:22:32.230 --> 00:22:35.020
you can easily undelete a
table for up to three days

00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:36.350
with a single SQL command.

00:22:36.350 --> 00:22:39.550
So there's no need to run to the
DBA and ask for a tape backup

00:22:39.550 --> 00:22:41.410
and have your heart
skip a beat as you

00:22:41.410 --> 00:22:44.210
think about the implications
of what's happened there.

00:22:44.210 --> 00:22:48.740
BigQuery handles all of that for
us in a really easy to use way.

00:22:48.740 --> 00:22:50.440
And finally, with
respect to cost,

00:22:50.440 --> 00:22:53.500
again, the on-demand pricing
allowed us to start things up

00:22:53.500 --> 00:22:54.970
at a low run rate.

00:22:54.970 --> 00:22:56.950
And then once we found
what we stabilized at,

00:22:56.950 --> 00:22:58.803
we could basically
purchase reserve slots

00:22:58.803 --> 00:23:00.970
and make sure that our cost
was fixed going forward,

00:23:00.970 --> 00:23:03.652
that we wouldn't
have sudden spikes.

00:23:03.652 --> 00:23:05.360
So now I'm going to
jump into a live demo

00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:08.330
where I'm going to show how
we democratized data at MLB.

00:23:08.330 --> 00:23:10.580
And in this case, I'm going
to walk through an example

00:23:10.580 --> 00:23:13.220
where we are going to take a
data set that contains data

00:23:13.220 --> 00:23:15.740
for all 30 Major
League Baseball clubs

00:23:15.740 --> 00:23:18.480
and show how we would share it
with just one of those clubs

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:21.470
while applying security to
ensure that that club can only

00:23:21.470 --> 00:23:23.032
see their own data.

00:23:23.032 --> 00:23:25.490
I'm going to jump in from the
perspective of a single club,

00:23:25.490 --> 00:23:27.320
in this case the
Yankees, and show

00:23:27.320 --> 00:23:30.170
how that club is able
to access that data,

00:23:30.170 --> 00:23:32.180
and even run queries
with their own data

00:23:32.180 --> 00:23:35.770
combining and joining
with that data.

00:23:35.770 --> 00:23:39.250
To demonstrate how BigQuery's
data set sharing and authorized

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:42.610
views allowed us to democratize
data access at Major League

00:23:42.610 --> 00:23:46.460
Baseball, I'm going to walk
through a small example here.

00:23:46.460 --> 00:23:49.240
I'm in the BigQuery console,
logged in as a Major League

00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:50.380
Baseball employee.

00:23:50.380 --> 00:23:53.740
So I have access to data
across all 30 teams.

00:23:53.740 --> 00:23:56.860
For example, here I've set
up a small dummy schema

00:23:56.860 --> 00:24:00.400
of tickets fact table that
contains one row per ticket

00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:02.650
sale transaction.

00:24:02.650 --> 00:24:04.600
As Major League
Baseball, we have access

00:24:04.600 --> 00:24:06.770
to all 30 teams' data.

00:24:06.770 --> 00:24:08.470
And one of our goals
here is to ensure

00:24:08.470 --> 00:24:10.810
that we share that data
with each of the 30 clubs

00:24:10.810 --> 00:24:13.700
so that they can make their
own local business decisions.

00:24:13.700 --> 00:24:15.880
So the goal would be to
share the Giants data

00:24:15.880 --> 00:24:17.710
with just the Giants
and the White Sox data

00:24:17.710 --> 00:24:19.390
with just the White Sox.

00:24:19.390 --> 00:24:21.490
In our first iteration of
Wheelhouse data sharing,

00:24:21.490 --> 00:24:26.210
we accomplish this by setting up
30 copies of the data each day.

00:24:26.210 --> 00:24:29.920
So we would run queries
to segment out the data

00:24:29.920 --> 00:24:33.077
and pull all of the Giants
data and put that in one file.

00:24:33.077 --> 00:24:34.660
Then we'd pull data
for the White Sox,

00:24:34.660 --> 00:24:36.190
and put that in another file.

00:24:36.190 --> 00:24:38.740
This involved making
copies of the data, which

00:24:38.740 --> 00:24:42.430
incurs issues when perhaps you
have data that has been updated

00:24:42.430 --> 00:24:45.400
later on after the file
was produced perhaps,

00:24:45.400 --> 00:24:47.320
other data synchronization
issues, and just

00:24:47.320 --> 00:24:50.800
other challenges in making sure
that you stay in sync with what

00:24:50.800 --> 00:24:51.970
you have copied out.

00:24:51.970 --> 00:24:53.938
So with BigQuery, this
gets a lot easier.

00:24:53.938 --> 00:24:55.480
So to demonstrate
this, first of all,

00:24:55.480 --> 00:24:56.938
logged in as Major
League Baseball,

00:24:56.938 --> 00:25:00.160
I can do a demo query to
show that I can run something

00:25:00.160 --> 00:25:04.660
like a sum of the quantity from
this table across all 30 teams.

00:25:04.660 --> 00:25:06.730
So as Major League
Baseball, I can

00:25:06.730 --> 00:25:09.430
see that some of the quantity
broken down by the team name,

00:25:09.430 --> 00:25:11.050
for example.

00:25:11.050 --> 00:25:12.550
And we can see that
I do have access

00:25:12.550 --> 00:25:16.070
here as Major League Baseball
to all 30 of the clubs' data.

00:25:16.070 --> 00:25:18.868
So now if I want to just
share data with the Yankees,

00:25:18.868 --> 00:25:20.410
for example, we
should be giving them

00:25:20.410 --> 00:25:23.180
access to just the slice of
the data pertinent to them.

00:25:23.180 --> 00:25:25.420
The way we accomplish
that is by creating a data

00:25:25.420 --> 00:25:27.640
set that we share data
with the Yankees in.

00:25:27.640 --> 00:25:29.460
Assuming one doesn't
already exist,

00:25:29.460 --> 00:25:32.500
I can go into BigQuery's
console and simply set up

00:25:32.500 --> 00:25:36.002
a data set that we will use
for sharing with the Yankees.

00:25:36.002 --> 00:25:38.210
And within that data set,
I'm going to create a view.

00:25:38.210 --> 00:25:40.343
And this is a standard
SQL view that's

00:25:40.343 --> 00:25:42.010
just going to say,
we're going to create

00:25:42.010 --> 00:25:46.870
a view of the next_demo_Yankees
tickets as select star

00:25:46.870 --> 00:25:52.602
from the parent data set, where
the team name is the Yankees.

00:25:52.602 --> 00:25:54.310
So we're just going
to say where the team

00:25:54.310 --> 00:25:56.420
name is equal to Yankees.

00:25:56.420 --> 00:25:59.080
So this is a standard SQL view.

00:25:59.080 --> 00:26:00.830
And we'll see that
it gets created.

00:26:00.830 --> 00:26:03.670
And we can check it out that the
DBL is available here for us.

00:26:03.670 --> 00:26:07.540
And it's our pretty
traditional SQL view,

00:26:07.540 --> 00:26:11.260
where it just gives access
to that slice of the data.

00:26:11.260 --> 00:26:13.900
Most data warehouse systems
and database systems do not

00:26:13.900 --> 00:26:15.940
allow you to share
access to the view

00:26:15.940 --> 00:26:18.400
without sharing access
to the underlying table.

00:26:18.400 --> 00:26:21.100
And this is where BigQuery's
authorized view differs.

00:26:21.100 --> 00:26:22.810
So normally, I couldn't
share this view

00:26:22.810 --> 00:26:25.393
without actually giving access
to the entire base table, which

00:26:25.393 --> 00:26:26.810
we wouldn't want
to do, because we

00:26:26.810 --> 00:26:28.420
want to give the
Yankees only access

00:26:28.420 --> 00:26:30.760
to their subset of the data.

00:26:30.760 --> 00:26:33.550
So all we have to do here to
enable this functionality is

00:26:33.550 --> 00:26:36.667
on our parent data set,
set up the authorized view

00:26:36.667 --> 00:26:39.250
to say that this new view that
we've created in the child data

00:26:39.250 --> 00:26:41.955
set is authorized to
access the parent data set.

00:26:41.955 --> 00:26:43.330
So in this case
here, we're going

00:26:43.330 --> 00:26:45.280
to say that the
next_demo_Yankees view is

00:26:45.280 --> 00:26:48.320
authorized to access
the next_demo_sales,

00:26:48.320 --> 00:26:49.810
the parent data set.

00:26:49.810 --> 00:26:52.060
With that in place,
all I now need to do

00:26:52.060 --> 00:26:54.520
is figure out who I want
to share the data with.

00:26:54.520 --> 00:26:58.180
And this works very similarly
to Google Drive or Google Docs

00:26:58.180 --> 00:27:00.760
sharing, where you simply
provide a list of G Suite email

00:27:00.760 --> 00:27:02.830
addresses or G Suite groups.

00:27:02.830 --> 00:27:06.370
For this demo, I'm going to be
using just a single dummy email

00:27:06.370 --> 00:27:07.450
address I set up.

00:27:07.450 --> 00:27:10.360
But in production, we
actually use G Suite groups

00:27:10.360 --> 00:27:12.730
to enable entire groups of
users at a time that are

00:27:12.730 --> 00:27:15.110
managed locally by the clubs.

00:27:15.110 --> 00:27:16.570
So all I have to
do to share data

00:27:16.570 --> 00:27:18.070
is give it a list
of email addresses

00:27:18.070 --> 00:27:19.420
that I want to share with.

00:27:19.420 --> 00:27:21.100
So I'll use my dummy
email address here

00:27:21.100 --> 00:27:24.460
of yankeestaff@gmail.com.

00:27:24.460 --> 00:27:26.710
And I tell it that I
want to give access

00:27:26.710 --> 00:27:28.480
just to view the data,
not to modify it,

00:27:28.480 --> 00:27:30.010
not to modify the DBL.

00:27:30.010 --> 00:27:32.600
It's a read-only access
that I'm providing here.

00:27:32.600 --> 00:27:36.160
And much like any other Google
Suite sharing, that's it.

00:27:36.160 --> 00:27:36.970
We're done.

00:27:36.970 --> 00:27:39.940
So now we can see that we
immediately have given access

00:27:39.940 --> 00:27:42.457
to a set of users or a user.

00:27:42.457 --> 00:27:44.040
So I'm going to
switch gears here now.

00:27:44.040 --> 00:27:45.550
I'm going to switch over
to my browser window

00:27:45.550 --> 00:27:46.967
to see what the
Yankees would see.

00:27:46.967 --> 00:27:48.430
I'm logged in here
on this browser

00:27:48.430 --> 00:27:50.117
window as the Yankees staff.

00:27:50.117 --> 00:27:51.700
And initially, I
would have had access

00:27:51.700 --> 00:27:54.070
to perhaps nothing, if nothing
had been shared with me

00:27:54.070 --> 00:27:56.450
yet from Major League
Baseball, essentially.

00:27:56.450 --> 00:27:58.240
But if I refresh
my screen now, I

00:27:58.240 --> 00:28:00.760
have access right
away to that data set

00:28:00.760 --> 00:28:02.000
that's been given to me here.

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:03.880
So I have this
tickets data set here.

00:28:03.880 --> 00:28:05.570
And I can start running
queries on it right away.

00:28:05.570 --> 00:28:07.570
For example, the same sum
of the quantity that I

00:28:07.570 --> 00:28:09.940
was running on the
parents data set,

00:28:09.940 --> 00:28:11.658
I can run here all the same.

00:28:11.658 --> 00:28:13.450
And I'll see here that
as the Yankees user,

00:28:13.450 --> 00:28:15.700
I'm only getting access to
that slice of data that's

00:28:15.700 --> 00:28:17.300
pertinent to the Yankees.

00:28:17.300 --> 00:28:20.830
So in this case here, it's
just that 10,000 rows.

00:28:20.830 --> 00:28:22.360
What makes this
even more powerful

00:28:22.360 --> 00:28:24.490
is that I can bring
my own data that I

00:28:24.490 --> 00:28:27.220
may have locally and not shared
with Major League Baseball.

00:28:27.220 --> 00:28:29.170
And I can join that
across these data

00:28:29.170 --> 00:28:31.930
sets so there's no
barriers here of data silos

00:28:31.930 --> 00:28:34.240
where datas in different
data sets can't be joined.

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:37.050
If you have access to data, no
matter what the data set is,

00:28:37.050 --> 00:28:38.620
you can join across it.

00:28:38.620 --> 00:28:41.010
So for my example
here, let's imagine

00:28:41.010 --> 00:28:43.050
the Yankees have a
small local sheet

00:28:43.050 --> 00:28:44.477
of promotions they are running.

00:28:44.477 --> 00:28:46.560
And they want to analyze
which of those promotions

00:28:46.560 --> 00:28:48.030
generated the most sales.

00:28:48.030 --> 00:28:51.090
So if this is a sheet
they may have in a CSV,

00:28:51.090 --> 00:28:53.900
they could go ahead
and use BigQuery's UI

00:28:53.900 --> 00:28:56.860
to very easily upload
that data from the CSV.

00:28:56.860 --> 00:28:58.822
We point to that CSV.

00:28:58.822 --> 00:29:00.030
We give it a table name here.

00:29:00.030 --> 00:29:01.202
We'll call it promos.

00:29:01.202 --> 00:29:02.910
And we'll tell it to
automatically detect

00:29:02.910 --> 00:29:04.110
the schema.

00:29:04.110 --> 00:29:06.660
And just like that, within
a few moments there,

00:29:06.660 --> 00:29:09.007
we will have a table
created for us locally.

00:29:09.007 --> 00:29:10.590
This would be a table
that the Yankees

00:29:10.590 --> 00:29:14.140
have access to that otherwise
is not shared anywhere else.

00:29:14.140 --> 00:29:17.490
Now a join could be written
that combines those two

00:29:17.490 --> 00:29:18.190
things together.

00:29:18.190 --> 00:29:19.650
So I've written
this query up here

00:29:19.650 --> 00:29:21.420
that says, let's take the
data that Major League

00:29:21.420 --> 00:29:22.440
Baseball would
have shared with us

00:29:22.440 --> 00:29:24.120
and the tickets
data set, and we're

00:29:24.120 --> 00:29:27.390
going to do a join with the
promos data on the game date.

00:29:27.390 --> 00:29:29.610
And this will allow us
to run that query that

00:29:29.610 --> 00:29:31.170
crosses those two
data sets and sees

00:29:31.170 --> 00:29:34.197
which promotion has the
highest overall quantity sold.

00:29:34.197 --> 00:29:36.780
So in this example here, we see
that the highest quantity sold

00:29:36.780 --> 00:29:38.070
was a t-shirt.

00:29:38.070 --> 00:29:39.570
That led to the
most quantity sold,

00:29:39.570 --> 00:29:41.700
other than no promotions,
which were there

00:29:41.700 --> 00:29:44.342
because perhaps we didn't
fill in all the promo data.

00:29:44.342 --> 00:29:46.050
So you can see how
this paradigm of being

00:29:46.050 --> 00:29:47.550
able to join across
data sets you're

00:29:47.550 --> 00:29:51.120
authorized to allows you
to have different access

00:29:51.120 --> 00:29:54.420
patterns for data, while still
allowing centralized querying

00:29:54.420 --> 00:29:54.975
across them.

00:29:54.975 --> 00:29:56.100
RYAN MCDOWELL: Thanks, Rob.

00:29:56.100 --> 00:29:58.590
And thank you for joining
us for Google Cloud Next.

00:29:58.590 --> 00:30:01.220
Be sure to check out some of
our other great data analytics

00:30:01.220 --> 00:30:02.530
sessions.

00:30:02.530 --> 00:30:04.160
Thank you.

